## The Generic Vec

`Vec` itself is a generic type: `Vec[T]` where `T` can be ANY type. Every Vec you've used so far — `Vec[Int]`, `Vec[Str]` — is the same Vec type with a different type parameter.

### Vec of Different Types

```xiom
let ints: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
ints.push(1); ints.push(2);

let strs: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
strs.push("hello"); strs.push("world");

let bools: Vec[Bool] = Vec[Bool].new();
bools.push(true); bools.push(false);

let floats: Vec[Float64] = Vec[Float64].new();
floats.push(3.14); floats.push(2.71);
```

### Generic Functions on Any Vec

```xiom
fn count_items[T](v: Vec[T]) -> Int {
  return v.len();
}

fn has_items[T](v: Vec[T]) -> Bool {
  return !v.is_empty();
}

fn peek_last[T](v: Vec[T]) -> Option[T] {
  return v.get(v.len() - 1);
}
```

These functions work on `Vec[Int]`, `Vec[Str]`, `Vec[Bool]` — any Vec at all.

### Full Program

```xiom
fn print_all[T](v: Vec[T]) {
  for item in v { io.println(item); }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let bools: Vec[Bool] = Vec[Bool].new();
  bools.push(true); bools.push(false); bools.push(true);

  print_all(bools);
  io.println("Count: " + string.int_to_str(bools.len()));

  let floats: Vec[Float64] = Vec[Float64].new();
  floats.push(1.1); floats.push(2.2);

  print_all(floats);
  return 0;
}
```

### What T Can Be

- Built-in types: `Int`, `Float64`, `Str`, `Bool`, `Char`
- Custom types: `Vec[Person]`, `Vec[Card]`
- Nested generics: `Vec[Pair[Str,Int]]` — a Vec of Pairs
- Collections: `Vec[Vec[Int]]` — a Vec of Vecs (matrix)

